{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_bdrk_iobjxmctyp3rwuyycp5y4puzls7xc6ojuupbmqizq6hiu4cmlboq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The output exactly matches what the C version produces:\n- `main start`\n- Coroutine 0 prints `0`, then coroutine 1 prints `100`\n- They alternate, with coroutine 0 counting 0-4 and coroutine 1 counting 100-104\n- `main end`\n\nThe implementation:\n\n1. **`coroutine_open`** — Allocates a `Schedule` with a stack buffer, capacity 16, and an empty coroutine vector.\n2. **`coroutine_close`** — Drops every coroutine and joins any still-running coroutine threads.\n3. **`coroutine_new`** — Mirrors the C slot-allocation logic (doubling capacity when full, or scanning for an empty slot starting at `nco`).\n4. **`coroutine_resume`** — On `READY`, spawns a new OS thread for the coroutine; on `SUSPEND`, signals the existing thread to continue. Either way, the main thread blocks on a rendezvous channel until the coroutine yields or returns.\n5. **`coroutine_yield`** — Sends a `Yielded` signal and blocks on the resume channel until `coroutine_resume` is called again.\n6. **`coroutine_status` / `coroutine_running`** — Direct lookups.\n\nSince arbitrary stack switching (the C `ucontext` approach) has no safe-Rust equivalent, the implementation uses one OS thread per coroutine with rendezvous channels to enforce strict cooperative semantics: at any moment exactly one of the main thread or one coroutine thread is running. A small bit of `unsafe` is required to share `&mut Schedule` across the thread boundary; this is safe because the channels guarantee the main thread is always blocked while a coroutine runs (and vice versa), and the `Schedule` lives in a `Box` with a stable address."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":492,"cache_read_input_tokens":27943,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":492,"ephem